  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1003 PM MDT Fri Jul 24 2026\n\nCAM's are relatively similar for Saturday to what they depicted\nFriday, albeit with rain shoved a little closer to the Divide \nand less within the valleys. Regardless, we are expecting \nanother day of isolated to scattered showers and thunderstorms \nfor much of the region, particularly in the southern half of the\nCWA. This is in spite of us being at the anticipated low point \nfor PWAT's (which, in the grand scheme of things, is still above\nnormal for this time of year). Many thunderstorms will be \ncapable of localized heavy rain, small hail, and gusty outflow \nwinds. Without much shear though, storms are generally expected\nto be short-lived. By Sunday, the moisture plume will have \ndrifted back overhead, and as the ridge stagnates, so will that \npool of moisture, setting up a long stretch of repetitive active\nweather. Flow around the ridge is weak, and as such, storms are\nexpected to be slow moving. With this, vulnerable areas \n(including burn scars) will be prone to flash flooding and \ndebris flows throughout the next week. Also stagnating will be \nour daily high temperatures, sitting roughly 5 degrees above \nnormal and leaving many valley locations near or within the \ntriple digits.",
